{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1497076-attempting-to-figure-out-the-distribution-of-religion-in-the-world","id":1497076,"title":"Attempting to figure out the distribution of religion in the World","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Onyx Path Worlds","Scion"],"posts":[{"date":"2022-11-14T01:44:00-08:00","id":1497076,"title":"Attempting to figure out the distribution of religion in the World","content":"<i>Origin </i>(p.28-29) implies (if I understand it correctly) that religion in the World differs from that in reality in that attempts to spread monotheism are replaced by much more halfhearted and unsuccessful attempts to spread henotheism. This makes sense considering the premise (the gods aren't going to want their worship to be wiped out, after all), but the books mostly don't give much detail as to the specifics of what this has entailed, despite the fact that it seems likely to have significant influence on the game. My guess is that the writers wanted to leave it to storyguides to figure out, which makes sense, but I thought I'd start a thread trying to figure it out.<br /> <br /> My thoughts:<br /> - South, East and Southeast Asia, the Pacific, and Sub-Saharan Africa are (with some exceptions) relatively simple, since attempts to spread monotheistic religions there were recent enough that to the degree they've succeeded it's easy to see how things would have gone had they failed - most Indonesian Muslims are probably Hindu, the replacement of traditional religions with Christianity has presumably been less successful, and the CCP is probably more tolerant of religion since I can't imagine they'd miss what a bad idea antagonizing every god with a significant following in China was, but overall I imagine things are fairly recognizable. The section on the Deva in <i>Hero </i>mentions Pakistan, so it was clearly still founded in the World. I'm not clear on how to square this with Indian &quot;Muslims&quot; being almost all crypto-Hindus, which I would otherwise have assumed.<br /> <br /> - Religion in pre-Muslim Central Asia was (according to my extremely cursory research) a mixture of Tengrism and Zoroastrianism, with some Buddhism spreading in from the east. The other religions I'm aware of that are most likely to have gained significant presence in the area in the intervening time are Mat Syra Zemlya, Arabian polytheism and the worship of the Annuna, via the various empires that have conquered parts of the region. I do not know enough about Central Asia to have a good idea of the religious situation there beyond that.<br /> <br /> - A major question with the Middle East and Northern Africa is the extent to which Arabisation replaced local religions with Arabian polytheism. The most straightforwards approach here is to model the situation of Kemeticism and Marzeh on Coptic and Maronite Christianity, with Arabian polytheism in the place of Islam, but there's a case to be made that since the state religion in the World's version of the caliphates was still Islam rather than Arabian polytheism they would not have spread Arabian polytheism as effectively as their real world counterparts did Islam. The fact that Netjer, Ilhim and Annuna would have had stronger fatebindings in the region and would most likely have been more invested in preserving their faiths than the Arabian gods were in spreading theirs is another reason to expect the spread might have been less extreme, although it's hard to say by how much. It's also likely that the division between Arabian polytheists and the indigenous religions are much less clearcut than those between Islam and Christianity, as pre-Abrahamic Middle-Eastern religions were, to my understanding, much less concerned with preserving such boundaries.<br /> However, this is a place where the rules have something explicit to say, and it doesn't match with any of this. <i>Hero </i>(p.94) describes the Netjer as having only hundreds of thousands of remaining worshipers, but Wikipedia estimates there as being millions of Copts. It's not clear to me where this disparity is supposed to have come from. Did most Egyptians convert to Hellenismos in the post-Constantinian Roman empire? Were people less attached to Kemeticism than to Coptic Christianity? I can't really see anything that seems like a convincing explanation for why this would be.<br /> <br /> - I would imagine Helenismos still dominates Southern Europe - the only reason I can see that it wouldn't is if the Gothic invaders were much more successful at spreading Laukr in the World than they were at spreading Arianism in reality despite still being trying to spread Arianism rather than Laukr; Helenismos isn't as evangelical as Catholicism, so it's possible that the invading elites would have kept their Laukr religion and it would have spread from them to the masses. I don't see any case for the Ottoman empire spreading Tengrism more effectively than Islam.<br /> <br /> - Central Europe, Northern Europe and England are probably still predominantly Laukr, but I'm less confident there. Would Helenismos have spread the way Christianity did in reality? Would Helenismos have come to Germany via Charlemagne, or to England via William? Would the Italian-dominated medieval Vatican have zealously suppressed Laukr while turning a blind eye to Helenismos? Would the Renaissance idolization of ancient Greece and Rome have extended to adopting their religion? My guess is that while these would all have contributed to a significant presence of Helenismos in the region, the majority of the population still follow Laukr, but I'm not confident in this.<br /> <br /> - Iberia is its own kettle of fish, and given its influence on South America a fairly significant one. Religion in pre-Christian Iberia (again, according to my extremely cursory research) was a mix of Marzeh, Helenismos, Celtic religion and unique native beliefs, and later invasions would have brought Laukr, Arabian polytheism and the traditional Berber religion into the mix as well. We know from the rules that the Celtic religion was eventually wiped out, but beyond this, much as in the case of Central Asia, I would need a considerably better knowledge of the region's history than I in fact have to guess how the religious situation shakes out.<br /> <br /> - In Australia and North America, the population is predominantly descended from English colonists, who would presumably have brought their religions with them. I would expect this to lead to Laukr to be the most widespread religion, and to dominate things on a national level. However, the fatebinding of indigenous gods to the land, and the greater openness of Laukr to syncretism compared to Christianity, would likely mean a greater uptake of indigenous religion among the colonists that actually occurred. That attempts by the British colonists to wipe out native religions would have gone less successfully is a direct implication of the &quot;attempts to spread monotheism in the World have been much less successful&quot; premise. Indigenous religions thus likely have a significantly greater presence in the World's version of these regions than they do in reality.<br /> <br /> - Latin America is similar in having a large population of European colonists, but differs both in that they make up a smaller portion of the total population and in that the colonists were from Iberia, and thus likely more religiously diverse. In addition to these and the native population, there is a large Black population descended from West African slaves, whose religions would also have been brought with them.<br /> <br /> Thoughts?<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27668-mrred","name":"MrRed","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-11-14T06:26:38-08:00","id":1497090,"title":"","content":"This is one of the biggest hand-waves in the whole concept - &quot;All Myths Are True&quot;, but everything is essentially the same as it is in the &quot;real world&quot;. This is probably largly due to an effort on the designers' part not to reduce, trivialize, or offend existing religions, and partly because everyone has their own idea of how things would have worked, and so having an official world wasn't worth the effort.<br /> Oddly, what almost works better, is 1st edition's premise that the old Gods have been away, and are just coming back again. Then history can be as it is until now, and you can change it as you want going forward, when the pantheons return.<br /> But yes, it requires work on the GMs part, because players will ask all the questions.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":5627,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5627-purple-snit","user":"Purple Snit","date":". 11-14-2022, 12:49 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5627-purple-snit","name":"Purple Snit","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5627_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"309"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-11-14T07:34:32-08:00","id":1497092,"title":"","content":"The true is there is no real setting in Scion, there is even a guide to adjust mythic level (and many aspects) in the Companion exactly to allow you to create a setting that make sense.<br /> <br /> My suggestion is, to make it feels the same, add Monotheistic gods as gods in the same way as you have the other gods (or as a Primordial that is pretty distant but give some low level power to many mortals). You can have a bunch of prophets and saints doing this god\u2019s religion spreading, and people, like in our World will turn to this religion for what it is or by force. The other pantheons are still present, some very active, others not so much, but you will find people worshipping them everywhere (Orishas are pretty common in Brazil for example, I am talking real world Brazil), like the neo pagan religions in western countries and all the still active religions in eastern countries.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/en/product/481584/house-rules-bundle-bundle\" target=\"_blank\">House Rules - The Bundle</a> - House Rules for Everyone<br /> <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/en/product/481581/powered-inspiration-bundle\" target=\"_blank\">Powered Inspired</a> - Powered Characters using Inspiration and Gifts<br /> <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/413362/Fists-and-Tomes-BUNDLE\" target=\"_blank\">Fists and Tomes</a> - Inspired Martial Arts and Mysticism for Talents<br /> <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/en/product/492147/supertech\" target=\"_blank\">Supertech</a> - Super Science in the hands of Talents","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24985-mateus-luz","name":"Mateus Luz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24985_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2950"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-11-15T14:21:56-08:00","id":1497218,"title":"","content":"One thing that is clear is that the Abrahamic religions have been active in the setting and the Abrahamic myths are as true as any other. The chapter in <i>God</i> on antagonists (just released to backers today) explicitly states the &quot;God of the Israelites&quot; killed Leviathan. So, the non-Abrahamic religions might not have been marginalized as much as they have in the real world, but there's no reason the Abrahamic religions shouldn't have become influential.<br /> <br /> My own head-canon the matter:<br /> <br /> The God of Abraham straddles the line between a God and Primordial and has many Mantles, showing different faces to the faithful of different traditions. He does not intervene directly in the world, but acts through Messengers (Angels), Saints, and Prophets. He has no Scions, at least not any time recently. An idea I picked up from someone else is that sites holy to the God of Abraham--churches, mosques, synagogues and the like--negate the magic of all other Pantheons, explaining the distinctive power and appeal of his religions in the World without needing him to have Scions. I think the official position of Abrahamic religious leaders could be that the Gods are actually fallen angels, ones who did not side with Lucifer, but tried to remain neutral; they were cast out of Heaven, but only as far as Earth, not hell. (This was a real world explanation for the Fey.)<br /> <br /> In the real world, the word &quot;pagan&quot; comes from the Latin term for someone who dwelt in rural areas, because the old religions clung on longer there. We can kick that up a notch and say that they lingered and, in some cases, even thrived there throughout the Middle Ages, but the aristocracy and intellectuals were, for the most part, Christian or Muslim. Perhaps some places remained devoted to the old Gods--I could see Nubia remaining devoted to the Netjer, even after Egypt became mostly Muslim.<br /> <br /> Then, in the Renaissance, as intellectuals became interested in ancient philosophy and Hermeticism, there was a revival of interest among them in the old Gods as well, particularly the Theoi and Netjer. In the real world, this interest in ancient philosophy and Hermeticism happened, along with an accompanying intellectual interest in ancient Greco-Roman mythology and religion. During the 1800s--the era of romanticism, nationalism and Victorian occultism in the real world--there was a dramatic wave of revival of devotion to the old Gods, especially the Pantheons romantics and nationalists saw as fitting with the newly developing national identities. Occultists would have developed a strong interest in pantheons like the Netjer and Aesir whose PSPs involve magic. In the 1960s and 70s, which in our world saw the rise of neo-paganism, there would have been another wave of conversions to the old religions in the World.<br /> <br /> Many people in Christian and Muslim regions would probably honor the old gods alongside the God of Abraham, despite the official disapproval of Abrahamic religions leaders. After all, in the real world, lots of Christians and Muslims believe and practice all sorts of non-orthodox stuff like astrology or reincarnation.<br /> <br /> I think the World of Scion developing in parallel to our world is somewhat plausible, at least enough for game purposes--you just need to tweak some real world trends to make it work. The outcome looks both different and familiar, like what the game is going for.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26114-muskrat","name":"Muskrat","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"75"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-11-15T15:44:07-08:00","id":1497233,"title":"","content":"By the way, many Christians (John Milton for example) believed that the Pagan gods were powerful faeries. Real world Islam and it's belief in the Jinn show that Abrahamic faiths could embrace the existence of powerful spiritual beings not in the service of God or the Devil. So the idea of the Abrahamic faiths excepting the Gods as Fallen Angels/Jinn/Faeries has real world back up.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-11-22T16:33:38-08:00","id":1497865,"title":"","content":"My own take is that this sort of thing depends on how you adjust the Evidence knob. Iron looks like the real world, with the gods in hidden nooks and crannies. Gold is essentially modern fantasy with the Pantheons replacing a lot of modern cultural cues. <br /> <br /> I frequently think of the World as having been at Bronze level for at least the last third of the 20th century, with the current wave of Scions (including the PCs) and their adventures since the start of the 21st century having raised the World's Evidence level to Silver (what is supposed to be Scion's default setting). This includes an explosion of new worshipers of the various Pantheons and gods springing up all over the world. These cults need not be bound by history, race, or other factors. Just as an example, use the band Wu Tang Clan as the inspiration/basis for a Shen Cult in inner city America, mixing hip hop culture with ancestor worship, Neo-Confuscionism, and martial arts. Similarly, you might find sizable Cults devoted to the Mesopotamian gods in New York and London, the Theoi in Australia or Egypt, or the Loa on the Internet.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-12-05T05:16:48-08:00","id":1498837,"title":"","content":"I politely side step this by having the major Abrahamic religions still major cultural forces in their various areas, and the setting starting off as Iron, more or less. I had an initiating event trigger the return of Myth to The World at the edges (this was during my players Origins arc) with the ball starting to roll faster through following events and the players becoming more aware that something has been out there for a while. They just never noticed.<br /> <br /> While the upper crust of societies have been as cynical as ours is (excepting quiet cults working the gods various wills), those in the lower ends of various societies have been more aware, and have been working various Gods or Titans wills for a long time. And the triggering even has increased their power.<br /> <br /> I have rolled this into a major Cult threat referred to as the Harbingers of Ragnar\u00f6k, An over cult that draws from all of the Aesir Titans formed by a cadre of betrayed and disgruntled ex-military officers that too a look around and said, in essence, &quot;Corruption is everywhere. It ALL has to go. Burn it down.&quot; Most people would think that that means Coup to put themselves in power (like the players). Until they managed to capture a few of them. And were told to their faces exactly what they mean. and I had the Harbingers level Portland, WA to get the point across.<br /> <br /> As the players discover that the Harbingers of Ragnarok (HoR) have tentacles world wide, they have some some globe hopping but have run into some of the other pantheons in the rough areas they have historically have been (with some exceptions) and have worked to warn the locals if they are not aware of them. But they have found out the HoR use lots of locally oppressed populations as catspaws. As they have travelled and seen things and various mythical creatures, the setting has moved slowly towards Bronze.<br /> <br /> The players have scored a major victory recently by blowing up a central co-ordination base that the HoR have used, and also have run off with a fair amount of intel. Which included maps to various Axis Mundis and Overwold places the HoR are aware of and use as shipping paths to move around in.<br /> <br /> It's been a way I use to introduce the players to the setting, and slowly scale things up as they advance. What was once secret...not as much anymore.<br /> <br /> As to the various religions wandering around in the US, I tend to play it fairly low key, but lean on The Republic variation of the Theoi Pantheon, but their are the Roman (imperium) versions and the original that have quietly seeped it over the centuries. The U.S. Military definitely has more Roman aspects towards it, such as Triumph awards to victorious generals, The Agoge (Spartan military schools) are traditional military schools that turn out hardcore soldiers (they view West Point graduates with more than a bit of contempt). <br /> <br /> In short, I work small things in at the edges, enough to show major and minor differences, but enough to keep things relatively recognizable. <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13091-thewanderingjewels","name":"TheWanderingJewels","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13091_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"112"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-12-07T10:40:50-08:00","id":1499078,"title":"","content":"I always seemed to me, that Christianity exist exactly same way as in our world. I would just say, that it looks that most of people either wholy ignore first commandment or they found some syncretic workaround (like other gods being angels... so scions are probably nephilim seen in more tolerant version).<br /> Like even in real word, for example many people see no real issue in being christians and worshiping loa at same time (not to mention, that many loa probably share mantles with some ascended christian saints).<br /> So I would say, christian parts of world are still christian, it's just... Much less of christian see a problem in believing that Christ is not only god (or he is only god, but you still have that fallen angel Zeus breeding nephilims left and right and transforming anybody who offend him into something funny).<br /> Like is really question, what is a christianity itself, as according to working of the World, it doesn't make sense, for abrahamic god to not exist. So either it is some really weird anomaly (like inactive god mentioned above). Or there is christian pantheon... Like Christ really seems like ascended scion, so holy trinity probably isn't exactly truth, but it's quite plausible that he is scion of the Father, who ascended and currently share a mantle with the Father. Do not ask me about holy spirit, but he can be some third god sharing &quot;Holy trinity&quot; mantle. Also it isn't really problem to admit a godhood even to saints. Like they were making miracles, people worship them, so I see no problem in saying that they are a chosen scions. Also it looks that many saints share mantles with paganic gods. This is common with Loa, but also like celtic Brigid share mantle with saint Brigid. Not to mention that even Christ is syncretised with Guanyin (like yes, it happened in real world).<br /> Also I really wouldn't be surprised that the World would have many more syncretism connecting monotheistical faiths to other gods.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25570-ilicius","name":"Ilicius","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-12-07T11:52:47-08:00","id":1499084,"title":"","content":"Well, in Scion, all myths are true, so the versions of the God of Abraham in all the different Abrahamic traditions would be true simultaneously. And new Pantheons change reality retroactively, so Christ could have retroactively become the means through which the Trinity created the World.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26114-muskrat","name":"Muskrat","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"75"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-12-07T13:16:57-08:00","id":1499097,"title":"","content":"It was actually the Virgin Mary who was syncretized with Kannon, Guanyin's Mantle among the Kami. This was done during the period when it was illegal to practice Christianity in Japan, so having a figure who could equally well be Kannon or Mary became a way to venerate a Christian figure without openly venerating a Christian figure.<br /> <br /> My favorite bit of Christian lore that would take on new meaning in the World is that, in the earliest accounts of him, St. Christopher was a dog-headed giant. In the World of Scion, it's unlikely that would ever change--and it would be a way for the Church to show it could include Denizens. (In our world, the Catholic Church has come to the conclusion that St. Christopher was not a historical figure and quietly delisted him as an official saint.)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26114-muskrat","name":"Muskrat","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"75"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-12-08T07:45:01-08:00","id":1499144,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Muskrat</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1497076-attempting-to-figure-out-the-distribution-of-religion-in-the-world?p=1499097#post1499097\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It was actually the Virgin Mary...)</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm sorry, you are right. But it's not only Japan thing, as if I remember correctly it was used by Jesuit in China as a pathway for christianisation. As they could say, they already worship Virgin Mary, just under different name. Btw, that title Goddess of Mercy is Jesuit invention <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25570-ilicius","name":"Ilicius","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-12-08T07:49:19-08:00","id":1499145,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Muskrat</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1497076-attempting-to-figure-out-the-distribution-of-religion-in-the-world?p=1499084#post1499084\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Well, in Scion, all myths are true, so the versions of the God of Abraham in all the different Abrahamic traditions would be true simultaneously. And new Pantheons change reality retroactively, so Christ could have retroactively become the means through which the Trinity created the World.</div> </div> </div> </div>Like yes, I would say that Christ go really well with ascendency as described in Scion. He was &quot;son of God&quot;, then died in epic fashion, ascended on Heaven and retroactively became part of Trinity.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25570-ilicius","name":"Ilicius","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-12-10T12:18:37-08:00","id":1499303,"title":"","content":"I mean, the first commandment is often translated as something close to &quot;I am the LORD your God, who brought you out of the land of Egypt, out of the house of bondage. You shall have no other gods before Me&quot;, so maybe in this setting people interpret it as worshiping God first and then addressing the other gods second?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1006-widdershawns","name":"widdershawns","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1006_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"87"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-02-03T20:48:26-08:00","id":1502080,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Astromancer</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1497076-attempting-to-figure-out-the-distribution-of-religion-in-the-world?p=1497233#post1497233\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">By the way, many Christians (John Milton for example) believed that the Pagan gods were powerful faeries. Real world Islam and it's belief in the Jinn show that Abrahamic faiths could embrace the existence of powerful spiritual beings not in the service of God or the Devil. So the idea of the Abrahamic faiths excepting the Gods as Fallen Angels/Jinn/Faeries has real world back up.</div> </div> </div> </div>This, there are Christians who are open to the idea of other supernatural beings existing so long as you keep in mind that they are below God. Even now you hear about people who are worried that things like Harry Potter are going to teach their kids real magic, for example. I'm not sure if the ones who say 'nope, all supernatural phenomena must come from God and so none of these other pagan supernatural things ever actually existed at all' are even actually the majority, TBH.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Exalted Whose Name is Carved in Leaves of Jade<br /> Senator of the Greater Chamber<br /> Champion of the Silver Pact<br /> Chosen of the Little Gods<br /> Agent of Fate","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/988-mizu","name":"Mizu","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar988_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1520"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-02-22T05:45:10-08:00","id":1503675,"title":"","content":"We also must remember that the syncretism of the belief in supernatural beings in the Abrahamic religions has been around since the time of the ancient Israelite religion. They were originally polytheist and then became henotheist/monolatrists in worshipping only one single deity, but there were other deities who were inferior to their deity. Even after it embraced monotheism, it still believed that other spirits and entities existed, but they are all inferior to the true God. So, there is precedent for it existing in a Scion game.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":24121,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24121-starcomet","user":"Starcomet","date":". 02-22-2023, 09:50 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24121-starcomet","name":"Starcomet","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24121_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"96"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-02-22T06:36:03-08:00","id":1503680,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Starcomet</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1497076-attempting-to-figure-out-the-distribution-of-religion-in-the-world?p=1503675#post1503675\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">We also must remember that the syncretism of the belief in supernatural beings in the Abrahamic religions have been around since the time of the ancient Israelite religion. They were originally polytheist and then became henotheist/monolatrists in worshipping only one single deity, but there were other deities who were inferior to their deity. Even after it embraced monotheism, it still believed that other spirits and entities existed, but they are all inferior to the true God. So, there is precedent for it existing in a Scion game.</div> </div> </div> </div>Even in the Middle Ages Christians imagined the pagan gods were real. Some like Athena or Hermes were seen as powerful faeries. The Romance Parsforest describes Mars and Venus as &quot;subGods&quot; taking over certain duties from God.<br /> <br /> In the present day, Voudoun is famous for equating the Loa with Christian Saints.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":857,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","user":"Astromancer","date":". 02-20-2025, 07:13 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}